{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Pre-review citation sweep against current `origin/main`. Cites here are pinned to `083620b0`, now **333 commits** behind. The headline finding is not drift — it is that this story's stated motivation no longer holds.\n\n## 1. The 800-line premise is false, and AC4 is vacuous — BLOCKING\n\nContext/Problem states: \"`src/rebar/config.py` is exactly 800 lines on this basis — the hard cap in `.github/module-size-limit.txt`, enforced by the CI module-size gate with no allowlist — so this story must net-shrink it: the plan below lands the file at 778 lines.\"\n\nMeasured on `origin/main`: **`src/rebar/config.py` is 624 lines.** The cap in `.github/module-size-limit.txt` is indeed 800, so the file sits **176 lines under** it, not at it. Something between `083620b0` and now already shrank it by 176 lines.\n\nTwo consequences:\n\n- **AC4 is already satisfied and cannot fail.** \"`wc -l src/rebar/config.py` reports at most 778 lines\" is true of the untouched tree at 624. As written it proves nothing about this story's work and would pass against an empty diff. An AC that a no-op satisfies is not a criterion.\n- **The urgency argument is gone.** \"must net-shrink it\" was the stated reason the rewiring had to happen now. That pressure does not exist at 624 lines. The *other* reasons remain good on their own terms — the triplicated user-then-project discovery at what are now `config.py:268`, `config.py:587` and `show_config.py:93`, and the duplicated `_ordered_layers` + `from_mapping` composition — and those should carry the story. Recast Context/Problem around the duplication rather than the cap, and replace AC4 with something the change can actually move: a line-count **delta** against the pre-change file, or drop the count entirely and keep the complexity-ratchet half (`check_complexity_baseline.py --check` reporting `new=0 increased=0`), which is a real gate.\n\n## 2. AC1 is satisfiable, and its current hit set is this\n\n`git grep -n '_discover_project_config\\|user_config_path().is_file' -- src/rebar/config.py src/rebar/_commands/show_config.py` returns 4 hits today:\n\n- `src/rebar/config.py:42` — the import\n- `src/rebar/config.py:268` — inside `_ordered_layers`\n- `src/rebar/config.py:587` — inside `read_reserved_section`\n- `src/rebar/_commands/show_config.py:93` — inside `_raw_toml_layers`\n\nThe three call sites are exactly the ones the Approach rewires onto `raw_layers`, so the criterion is reachable. But AC1 says the grep should afterwards \"show only `_config_probe_paths`/`_validation_tokens`\", and **no current hit is inside `_config_probe_paths`** (it is at `:325`, and the grep does not land in it). `_validation_tokens` does not exist yet — the plan creates it. So AC1 describes a post-change shape that needs restating against what will actually remain: presumably the `:42` import plus whatever the new `_validation_tokens` uses. Make that explicit, or the AC is unverifiable as phrased.\n\n## 3. Line drift (substance intact — every symbol named exists)\n\n| Cited | Actual on `origin/main` |\n|---|---|\n| `_resolve` `:442-466` | `:347` |\n| `resolve_with_sources` `:774-800` | `:603` |\n| `_ordered_layers` `:330-359` | `:249` |\n| `load_config` `:469-519` | `:371` |\n| `read_reserved_section` `:739-762` | `:575` |\n| `_env_signature` | `:279` |\n| `_config_probe_paths` | `:325` |\n| discovery copy `config.py:343-355` | `:268` |\n| discovery copy `read_reserved_section:749-761` | `:587` |\n| discovery copy `show_config.py:_raw_toml_layers:86-102` | call at `:93` |\n| provenance loop `:791-799` | re-measure; `resolve_with_sources` is `:603` |\n| validation tokens `:451-466` | re-measure; `_resolve` is `:347` |\n| `_config_sources` import block `:43-87` | re-measure |\n| insert point \"after `_discover_project_config` (`:217`)\" | `_discover_project_config` is `_config_sources.py:166`; `user_config_path` is `:250` |\n\nNote the insert point specifically: the plan says to add `RawLayer`/`raw_layers` after `_discover_project_config` at `:217`, but that function is now at `:166` and `user_config_path` — which `raw_layers` also calls — is at `:250`, i.e. **after** the proposed insertion point. Placing `raw_layers` at `:166` would reference `user_config_path` before its definition. Module-level forward references are fine inside a function body, so this is probably harmless, but the stated position should be re-derived rather than assumed.\n\n`_operation_config.py:203-227,220,230-311`, `docs/reuse-surface.md:587-592`, `docs/config.md:88-99`, `tests/unit/test_config_perf.py:195-223,185-227`, `tests/unit/test_config_show.py:157-164`, and `tests/unit/test_llm_config_pointer.py:221-250` were **not** re-measured. Re-measure them with the rest before requesting review.\n\n## Base stability\n\nThis story sits behind `b939` → `cb2b` → `ed5a`. `cb2b` edits `src/rebar/config.py`, `src/rebar/_config_sources.py` and `src/rebar/_commands/show_config.py` — all three of this story's main files — and the Approach already accounts for that (\"the alias-story deletions ... land before this story\"). So these citations **will** move again. Re-measure after `cb2b` lands, not now; the finding in section 1 is the part that is worth acting on immediately, because it changes what the story is for."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793421631399765216,"uuid":"7de640c2-1117-4136-aa5e-9214060b2c5d"}